#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: production-v1
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: [move, callable]
# doc: |
#   RV-F #15 (single-owner Callable, REJECT direction — the bind position). A
#   `Callable` is single-owner (no implicit copy path in the lowering), so a BARE
#   identifier copy at a bind boundary must be rejected: write `!f` to move or
#   `f.clone()` to copy — E_MoveWithoutOperator. The oracle previously had no
#   bind-side reject for `Ty::Callable` and wrongly accepted this. This is the
#   canonical bare-assign site of production's single-owner-init carve-out; its
#   ACCEPT complement (`!f`-bind / `return f`) is callable_move_bind_return_ok.gg.
#
#   adjudicator: production-v1 — the definition rejects at the ELABORATE stage (a
#   `FrontendError`), so the ggdef run-surface lane records GGDEF-SKIP and
#   `ggdef gen` cannot own the expect. The C and LLVM production lanes affirm the
#   reject at build time with error[E_MoveWithoutOperator].
#
#   KNOWN SELF-HOST GAP (filed, TODO.md): the self-host driver does NOT yet treat
#   `Callable` as single-owner at init boundaries — it ACCEPTS this bare bind and
#   runs it. The self-host lane therefore MISMATCHes this fixture until that gap is
#   fixed. The expected verdict here is what the language SHOULD do (reject), not
#   what the self-host currently does — the fixture EXPOSES the gap rather than
#   ratifying the wrong behavior.
# expect:
#   exit: 1
#   stdout: ""
#   reject: E_MoveWithoutOperator
#!end

void main():
    Callable[int(int)] f = (n): n * 2
    Callable[int(int)] g = f
    print(g(5))
